  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 PM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\n*** Today through Friday Night ***\n\nSurface high pressure will persist over the region for the remaining \nof the week. Afternoon temperatures will slightly increase the next \ncouple days into the low to mid 80s across the area. Daytime mixing \nwill continue to mix drier air down to the surface dropping RH \nvalues. Minimum RH values for the next several days are expected to \nbe in the mid to low 30s. Areas with higher mixing heights have the \npotential for RH to drop into the upper 20s. \n\nA weakening frontal boundary will make its way to the region from \nthe north on Thursday. High pressure will build back in behind the \nfront leading to very little effect to the area. Temperatures in the \nBluegrass and southern IN areas will see minimal relief with highs \nin the upper 70s and low 80s. \n\n*** Saturday through Monday ***\n\nA tropical disturbance that will likely strengthen into a cyclone in \nthe Gulf of America is expected to track into CONUS and bring rain \nchances to the region. There is still uncertainties with the track \nand strength of this system, therefore low confidence in the details \nfor the weekend. Guidance is displaying two main outcomes for the \narea that is dependent on the systems track, timing, and synoptic \nfeatures. \n\nA shortwave disturbance that develops over the southern Plains will \npush NE toward the Ohio Valley on Saturday. The southern flow aloft \nwill begin to advect moisture from the Gulf into the region with the \nhelp of the shortwave. With the ample moisture and disturbance \nforcing, this will bring rain showers to area. However, some \nguidance shows this shortwave phasing in with the low pressure \nsystem after landfall. In this case, rainfall would be arriving with \nthe pressure system, which would have a later onset time in the \nlower Ohio Valley. This outcome would also result in a pressure \ngradient over the area bringing gusty winds. Generally, the \nconsistencies lie with onset time late Saturday into Sunday, with \nrain chances all day Sunday. QPF values are ranging from 0.5\" - 1\" \nacross the area. High-end amounts are closer to 2\" with swaths of \n2.5\" through central KY. \n\nAfter the disturbance moves east of the area, upper level ridging \nwill be west of the area, keeping a NW flow aloft. Temperatures to \nstart the week are expected to drop back into the low to mid 70s.",
